1
00:00:15,927 --> 00:00:28,972
[SPEAKER_01]: This particular sutta today, it's from the Anguttara Nikaya, and it's called Assemblies, and it's about community, community harmony, monks.

2
00:00:30,552 --> 00:00:33,733
These are the three communities or assemblies.

3
00:00:34,093 --> 00:00:34,534
[SPEAKER_01]: What three?

4
00:00:35,374 --> 00:00:41,696
[SPEAKER_01]: A community of the best, a divided community, and a harmonious community.

5
00:00:43,237 --> 00:00:44,337
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is a

6
00:00:44,817 --> 00:00:46,238
[SPEAKER_01]: A community of the best.

7
00:00:47,398 --> 00:00:56,684
[SPEAKER_01]: A community where the senior monks, the elders of the community, are not indulgent or slack, nor are they backsliders.

8
00:00:57,384 --> 00:01:07,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead, they take the lead in seclusion, rousing energy for the attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized.

9
00:01:08,870 --> 00:01:12,092
[SPEAKER_01]: And those who come afterwards follow their example.

10
00:01:13,393 --> 00:01:17,275
[SPEAKER_01]: They too are not indulgent or slack, nor are they backsliders.

11
00:01:17,875 --> 00:01:23,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead, they take the lead in seclusion, browsing energy for the attaining of the unattained.

12
00:01:23,839 --> 00:01:27,521
[SPEAKER_01]: This is called the assembly of the best, the community of the best.

13
00:01:31,042 --> 00:01:33,704
[SPEAKER_01]: And the... what is the divided community?

14
00:01:34,244 --> 00:01:41,488
[SPEAKER_01]: A community where the monks argue, quarrel, fight continually, wounding each other with

15
00:01:49,261 --> 00:01:51,983
[SPEAKER_01]: This is called the divided community.

16
00:01:53,524 --> 00:01:55,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the harmonious community?

17
00:01:56,606 --> 00:01:58,888
[SPEAKER_01]: A community where the monks live in harmony.

18
00:01:59,909 --> 00:02:11,218
[SPEAKER_01]: They are peaceable, uncontentious, or mudita with each other, without quarreling, blending like milk and water, and regarding each other with kindly eyes.

19
00:02:11,865 --> 00:02:14,026
This is the harmonious assembly.

20
00:02:17,328 --> 00:02:18,669
And just, just the last bit.

21
00:02:19,029 --> 00:02:23,171
[SPEAKER_01]: When the monks live in harmony like this, they make much merit.

22
00:02:23,631 --> 00:02:27,934
[SPEAKER_01]: At that time, the monks live in a heavenly situation.

23
00:02:28,134 --> 00:02:30,255
That's my translation for the Brahma-vihāra.

24
00:02:30,555 --> 00:02:32,376
They live in a heavenly situation.

25
00:02:33,237 --> 00:02:39,180
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the liberated mind of non-contention or

26
00:02:42,845 --> 00:02:44,866
So, there's a there's a

27
00:03:16,105 --> 00:03:29,436
[SPEAKER_01]: When you have a harmonious community, whether it's, you know, monastic or just a householder, you know, your family, any community, how to have harmony, how does one go about that?

28
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:44,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And for me, it seems to suggest there that when I think about harmony, you know, you yourself have to be

29
00:03:47,831 --> 00:03:51,873
Practicing harmonious qualities.

30
00:03:52,313 --> 00:03:58,936
You can't be doing things which give rise to trouble for yourself or others, i.e.

31
00:03:59,216 --> 00:04:02,117
unharmonious qualities, unwholesome qualities.

32
00:04:02,137 --> 00:04:11,381
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be doing that and expecting some basic unity within yourself or outside of yourself.

33
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:12,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, possible.

34
00:04:12,741 --> 00:04:12,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

35
00:04:13,781 --> 00:04:21,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, so, first thing is, like, people often confuse obedience for harmony.

36
00:04:23,290 --> 00:04:25,191
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not necessarily harmonious, you know.

37
00:04:25,432 --> 00:04:36,080
[SPEAKER_00]: People might not voice their disagreement due to one reason or the other, you know, authoritative setup or something, but that doesn't mean they regard each other with friendly eyes.

38
00:04:38,401 --> 00:04:39,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

39
00:04:39,262 --> 00:04:47,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so then, you ask yourself, or anybody can ask themselves, what is the, what is the true cause of this harmony?

40
00:04:48,609 --> 00:04:49,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it behavior of others?

41
00:04:51,051 --> 00:04:52,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it ill will in my mind?

42
00:04:54,232 --> 00:05:03,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the mental pressure of annoyance and aversion that I cannot live with?

43
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:11,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I am addressing the circumstances and trying to change the world or people around me, so I don't have to go through these mental states?

44
00:05:14,289 --> 00:05:15,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that the reason for disharmony?

45
00:05:16,130 --> 00:05:22,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it the genuine things that other people do and then we just need to fix their behavior and we'll live in perfect harmony?

46
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:25,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, the answer is quite evident.

47
00:05:26,861 --> 00:05:28,361
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that's the reason, really.

48
00:05:29,421 --> 00:05:36,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason for the disharmony in any community, including the world itself, it's the minds of individuals.

49
00:05:37,603 --> 00:05:43,344
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that's hard to... it's not that hard to see, but it's kind of hard to admit.

50
00:05:44,604 --> 00:05:47,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... well, first of all, it's painful.

51
00:05:48,545 --> 00:05:49,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind aversion is painful.

52
00:05:50,005 --> 00:05:51,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to get rid of it.

53
00:05:52,484 --> 00:06:01,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Second of all, you do also want to see an immediate result of your effort towards harmony or whatever else.

54
00:06:02,828 --> 00:06:11,131
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're not gonna see an immediate result if you are the only one just basically addressing your mind while everybody else seems to not care about it.

55
00:06:11,792 --> 00:06:15,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you realize, oh, but that's something you are also getting annoyed over.

56
00:06:16,253 --> 00:06:20,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one way or the other, you end up acting out of annoyance.

57
00:06:20,875 --> 00:06:22,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what other people do.

58
00:06:23,516 --> 00:06:26,337
[SPEAKER_00]: They act out of their annoyances and that's why there is no harmony.

59
00:06:27,118 --> 00:06:39,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about fabricating, you know, using psychotherapeutical methods and stuff to regard each other with friendly eyes and just visualize the friendship.

60
00:06:39,203 --> 00:06:39,323
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

61
00:06:39,463 --> 00:06:42,985
[SPEAKER_00]: It's removing your own resistance to your own mind of ill-will.

62
00:06:44,145 --> 00:06:45,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Overcoming your own ill-will

63
00:06:46,137 --> 00:06:49,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you will see everybody as a friend, basically, in that sense.

64
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,903
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be able to hate them.

65
00:06:53,984 --> 00:07:00,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, if every individual in that assembly does that, that is a perfect, that is a divine community.

66
00:07:00,610 --> 00:07:12,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Because every individual addressed the root of the problem, as opposed to ignore the root of the problem, acted out of the root of the problem, and changed the world the way he thinks it should be.

67
00:07:15,345 --> 00:07:24,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's assembly of three people, or five people, or the entire world, the root of the issue is always greed, aversion, delusion.

68
00:07:25,934 --> 00:07:30,238
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, like, oh, well, it's not going to happen that everybody will address that.

69
00:07:30,318 --> 00:07:37,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I better then, kind of, attack others, or try to change others, and get a shortcut to the solution of the problem.

70
00:07:38,065 --> 00:07:41,328
[SPEAKER_00]: But failing to understand that problem is still there.

71
00:07:42,783 --> 00:07:50,589
[SPEAKER_00]: The framework of the problem... the framework of the problem, greed, aversion, delusion, has remained unchanged because you just fed it and acted out of it.

72
00:07:51,950 --> 00:07:56,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's pretty much this, like, a symptom of when people want to change the world.

73
00:07:57,215 --> 00:08:02,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing themselves, they carry those issues they're complaining against and trying to change in others.

74
00:08:03,119 --> 00:08:04,060
[SPEAKER_00]: They carry in themselves.

75
00:08:04,420 --> 00:08:11,306
[SPEAKER_00]: But they kind of act out of conviction... conviction that as long as they change the world first, then they'll be all right as well.

76
00:08:12,210 --> 00:08:12,710
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

77
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:14,252
[SPEAKER_00]: It's putting the cart in front of the horse.

78
00:08:15,313 --> 00:08:16,073
[SPEAKER_00]: First things first.

79
00:08:16,433 --> 00:08:18,015
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's your own greed, aversion, delusion.

80
00:08:18,635 --> 00:08:19,376
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

81
00:08:19,456 --> 00:08:25,721
[SPEAKER_00]: An arahant, a person who has completely uprooted greed, aversion, delusion, has practiced for the benefit of the world as well.

82
00:08:26,682 --> 00:08:28,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he doesn't care about the world.

83
00:08:28,963 --> 00:08:37,470
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is, there is one individual that has uprooted the source of all problems of the entire saṃsāra, of everything.

84
00:08:38,371 --> 00:08:40,713
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the greatest contribution to the world you can make.

85
00:08:42,737 --> 00:08:52,079
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you can legislate, you can agree, you can learn the modes of behavior and etiquette, how we relate to each other, so, you know, so we don't argue and so on.

86
00:08:52,179 --> 00:09:05,022
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, the community, the wellness of the community, is measured by, you know, it's the wellness, the healthiness of individuals, the mental health of individuals.

87
00:09:05,782 --> 00:09:07,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they free from greed, aversion, delusion?

88
00:09:08,768 --> 00:09:09,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are they not?

89
00:09:09,848 --> 00:09:11,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they acting on account of a social agreement?

90
00:09:12,349 --> 00:09:16,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Or on account of simply not being able to have ill will towards each other?

91
00:09:17,471 --> 00:09:20,092
[SPEAKER_00]: And hence, that's a divine community.

92
00:09:20,112 --> 00:09:31,896
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not... you're not gonna reach... you're not... sorry... you're not gonna reach a divine community by... by, you know, creating a perfect social agreement, that we all share the same values and ethical convictions and so on.

93
00:09:32,336 --> 00:09:34,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but mentally, still greed, aversion, delusion.

94
00:09:34,357 --> 00:09:36,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, greed, aversion, delusion will have to.

95
00:09:37,867 --> 00:09:41,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Find its way in a negative... in a negative manner, because it's still there.

96
00:09:42,170 --> 00:09:46,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Because our social agreement is rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, because we didn't want to deal with the mind.

97
00:09:47,013 --> 00:09:57,220
[SPEAKER_00]: We wanted to address the society and then have things in order that way, which is kind of immediately satisfying and more very quick... quicker to verify and more pleasing.

98
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:03,764
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, we were acting out of our desires and wanted to satisfy our desire for a perfect order, for what we think the perfect order is.

99
00:10:04,745 --> 00:10:07,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But the root of the problem is the desire.

100
00:10:07,707 --> 00:10:09,569
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless that changes...

101
00:10:09,589 --> 00:10:09,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Individually.

102
00:10:10,930 --> 00:10:11,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

103
00:10:12,030 --> 00:10:12,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Individual desire.

104
00:10:12,951 --> 00:10:15,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless that changes, framework remains the same.

105
00:10:16,273 --> 00:10:19,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem has not been resolved.

106
00:10:19,236 --> 00:10:20,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Just permutated.

107
00:10:21,978 --> 00:10:31,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, if you do... if you claim to care for others, then, you know, you really should see

108
00:10:32,542 --> 00:10:36,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It is... you have to now remove those unwholesome things within yourself.

109
00:10:37,105 --> 00:10:39,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah, you can... you can care for others.

110
00:10:39,868 --> 00:10:49,515
[SPEAKER_00]: You can care for others, but then you ask yourself, yes, but am I caring out of desire towards others, towards order of the world that requires others?

111
00:10:50,458 --> 00:10:53,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, do I care because I am desireless?

112
00:10:53,179 --> 00:10:57,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I have no capacity for ill will, lust, or distraction.

113
00:10:57,861 --> 00:10:59,602
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I just simply wish people well.

114
00:11:00,282 --> 00:11:03,583
[SPEAKER_00]: On paper, both people care, but one is simply rooted in...

115
00:11:03,723 --> 00:11:09,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I care because I want to satisfy my desire of a perfect world that I like to think I'm living in, and so on.

116
00:11:10,046 --> 00:11:12,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not... that's not...

117
00:11:12,046 --> 00:11:15,808
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's not as unwholesome as not caring at all.

118
00:11:16,588 --> 00:11:21,774
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's certainly not wholesome on the level of uprooting your desire and really fixing the problem.

119
00:11:22,235 --> 00:11:28,583
[SPEAKER_00]: You kind of feel that, like, oh, well, if I just start uprooting my desires, there is nothing immediately verifying it.

120
00:11:29,063 --> 00:11:32,067
[SPEAKER_00]: The world seems to ignore my contribution.

121
00:11:32,607 --> 00:11:33,529
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no feedback.

122
00:11:33,549 --> 00:11:34,229
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing.

123
00:11:34,309 --> 00:11:35,070
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no reward.

124
00:11:35,130 --> 00:11:36,292
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no satisfaction.

125
00:11:37,535 --> 00:11:40,777
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it's just easier to just... yeah, but let's just go and change the world.

126
00:11:40,957 --> 00:11:50,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's unite us, all of us, and change the world for the better, while individually we act out of the same desires that those people who changed the world for the worst had.

127
00:11:50,902 --> 00:11:52,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the desire... that's what I said.

128
00:11:52,423 --> 00:11:53,883
[SPEAKER_00]: The desire is the most blameworthy.

129
00:11:55,144 --> 00:12:00,067
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have all sorts of, you know, lofty ethical standards, moral standards.

130
00:12:00,107 --> 00:12:02,548
[SPEAKER_00]: That's nice, but it's all well within saṃsāra.

131
00:12:02,608 --> 00:12:04,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes no difference to saṃsāra.

132
00:12:04,970 --> 00:12:23,306
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you talking from the point of view of simply having, kind of, you know, an agreeable, pleasant life in the current society, for which, you know, you don't need Buddha's instruction, just some common sense, or are you talking for, like, a genuine, you know, uprooting of the problem, fixing the individuals towards liberation?

133
00:12:23,866 --> 00:12:31,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Buddha compared that type of community to, like, community of Brahmas, because it's nothing short of that.

134
00:12:31,032 --> 00:12:32,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

135
00:12:33,023 --> 00:12:50,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in the worldly sense, you know, if an individual is practicing wholesome things, you know, restraining themselves, having some values in morality, and restraining themselves, that's already the community that will

136
00:12:53,723 --> 00:12:57,504
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, it is.

137
00:12:57,564 --> 00:12:59,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually, yeah, yeah.

138
00:12:59,225 --> 00:13:00,845
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's where the patience has to come in.

139
00:13:01,385 --> 00:13:19,972
[SPEAKER_00]: People, basically, people, people, like, even if they know that the root of the problem is a desire, they feel justified in aiming at the other people, at the society, at the arrangements, aiming at the, you know, that level of change, because they feel like, well, there is not enough time.

140
00:13:21,822 --> 00:13:29,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes too much time for me to be addressing my own, you know, desires and cravings, you know, tidy up my own room.

141
00:13:29,510 --> 00:13:31,032
[SPEAKER_00]: It just takes too much time for that.

142
00:13:31,472 --> 00:13:34,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's just go and tidy up a mess around us and then we'll tidy up our rooms.

143
00:13:34,756 --> 00:13:38,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But the mess around us is the mess of individual untidy rooms.

144
00:13:39,320 --> 00:13:39,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

145
00:13:40,915 --> 00:13:47,799
[SPEAKER_00]: But they feel like there is not enough time, so they feel justified in sort of addressing it on that projected level.

146
00:13:48,519 --> 00:13:51,301
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is, you're not addressing anything.

147
00:13:51,421 --> 00:13:57,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the problem of the mess is the problem that every individual brings through his own desires.

148
00:13:58,484 --> 00:13:59,065
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that's...

149
00:13:59,946 --> 00:14:03,788
[SPEAKER_00]: If that has not been addressed, means the framework for the mess remains.

150
00:14:03,828 --> 00:14:06,330
[SPEAKER_00]: You have just reordered it in a pleasing manner.

151
00:14:06,990 --> 00:14:09,552
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same mess.

152
00:14:10,152 --> 00:14:23,541
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, even in completely worldly terms, it would still actually matter if you not just address your own desires, tidy up your own room, but actually if that's the example you set.

153
00:14:24,777 --> 00:14:31,959
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you have like a, you know, back in the day they would have talked about it, when you have wholesome societies, when these values were shared among people.

154
00:14:32,759 --> 00:14:47,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So they were not confused, they were not impatient, they were not lying to themselves or convincing themselves that they should be, you know, addressing things on the level of the world, but they knew to take responsibility for their own desires, for their own behaviors, for their own virtue and morality.

155
00:14:48,084 --> 00:14:49,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the community was fine.

156
00:14:51,485 --> 00:14:54,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if every individual addresses its own domain,

157
00:14:55,084 --> 00:14:56,965
[SPEAKER_00]: The collection of those individuals, i.e.

158
00:14:57,065 --> 00:14:58,906
[SPEAKER_00]: the society, will have no issues.

159
00:15:01,668 --> 00:15:02,989
[SPEAKER_00]: But somebody has to start it.

160
00:15:03,329 --> 00:15:09,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially once the society has lost its ways, in terms of, you know, ethical standard and virtue and so on.

161
00:15:10,133 --> 00:15:14,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, somebody has to start re-educating, in that sense, through its own example.

162
00:15:15,497 --> 00:15:18,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through force or, like, brainwashing, basically.

163
00:15:18,839 --> 00:15:21,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's, again, you're rooted in your own desires.

164
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:22,581
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not changing anything.

165
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:28,323
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you want a united community, a harmonious community.

166
00:15:28,343 --> 00:15:30,925
[SPEAKER_01]: You must harmonize yourself.

167
00:15:31,225 --> 00:15:31,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

168
00:15:31,946 --> 00:15:41,173
[SPEAKER_00]: And that means you must remain within, or you must remain in internal harmony, even if others are not doing what you're doing.

169
00:15:41,954 --> 00:15:42,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

170
00:15:42,574 --> 00:15:47,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, as you say, such... there needs to be... someone needs to start that.

171
00:15:48,078 --> 00:15:49,139
[SPEAKER_01]: And such a person

172
00:15:50,372 --> 00:16:00,577
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, being amongst, you know, who has elders who are unwholesome, the whole society is unwholesome, the community, the mother, father, brothers and sisters, everyone's unwholesome.

173
00:16:01,337 --> 00:16:07,860
And then that one person decides, understands wholesome and sticks to that.

174
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:10,361
That is amazing.

175
00:16:10,901 --> 00:16:14,243
And then for a community to grow around that person, amazing.

176
00:16:14,623 --> 00:16:14,843
Yeah.

177
00:16:15,043 --> 00:16:17,144
And then there is... it's incredible.

178
00:16:18,453 --> 00:16:22,536
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha, for example, you know, a great example.

179
00:16:23,176 --> 00:16:28,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are other communities out there in the world that's happened presently and the past.

180
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:31,822
There's always had to be some person who had to do that.

181
00:16:31,862 --> 00:16:38,567
And then there were always elders in the community who had to keep on doing that.

182
00:16:39,188 --> 00:16:47,594
[SPEAKER_01]: And those elders have to, in a way, you have to realize the responsibility that you have, you know, as a parent,

183
00:16:48,310 --> 00:16:49,151
Yeah, yeah.

184
00:16:49,191 --> 00:16:54,414
I mean, you're doing it for yourself, basically.

185
00:16:54,874 --> 00:17:02,679
[SPEAKER_01]: You just, incidentally, it's contributing for the welfare of the future generations.

186
00:17:18,133 --> 00:17:21,214
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're doing it because you prefer to do it because...

187
00:17:21,254 --> 00:17:21,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

188
00:17:22,074 --> 00:17:31,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Through the practice of doing it, you have uprooted the desire, the aversion, and the unwholesome qualities, so now you actually prefer to stay in that mode.

189
00:17:31,555 --> 00:17:45,698
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no desire to return back to, you know, chasing desires and being projected out there onto the world and trying to change the circumstances and so on, which all kind of implies your desire one way or the other.

190
00:17:48,425 --> 00:18:05,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so that, like, just that responsibility, in a way, from the disciples of that teacher, or the one that learned from that elder, you know, if you want your community to be harmonious for generations to come, then, you know, you need to do that work internally.

191
00:18:06,429 --> 00:18:12,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but again, again, like, whether you want the community or not, you realize it doesn't matter.

192
00:18:13,622 --> 00:18:23,994
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have... even if you have no desire towards any community, if you abruptly desire... uprooted desire in yourself, that is setting the example whether you want it or not, whether you intend to do that or not.

193
00:18:25,596 --> 00:18:34,646
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're practicing wholesome things, developing wholesome qualities, whether you want to or not, harmony is the result.

194
00:18:35,198 --> 00:18:35,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

195
00:18:36,339 --> 00:18:40,564
[SPEAKER_00]: One way or the other, that is being perceived by others.

196
00:18:40,804 --> 00:18:50,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't necessarily make a mission out of, you know, showing the world and changing everybody through your own, you know, inner harmony and practice and virtue and so on.

197
00:18:50,976 --> 00:18:51,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It will stand out.

198
00:18:52,297 --> 00:18:53,538
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be for the benefit.

199
00:18:53,999 --> 00:18:55,841
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be for the benefit of the world.

200
00:18:59,813 --> 00:19:01,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

201
00:19:01,495 --> 00:19:02,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

202
00:19:11,314 --> 00:19:12,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

203
00:19:12,875 --> 00:19:20,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's pretty much that's the problem always, you know, like people generally today operate on the on that projected level of the world.

204
00:19:20,899 --> 00:19:24,200
[SPEAKER_00]: The views are on the level of the world, responsibilities on the level of the world.

205
00:19:24,881 --> 00:19:31,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Blame the world, you know, blame the governments, blame the this kind of, you know, more distant sense of others.

206
00:19:31,864 --> 00:19:32,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, it's other people.

207
00:19:32,545 --> 00:19:35,086
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, it's all rooted in the individuals.

208
00:19:36,562 --> 00:19:41,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you yourself are free from desire, you are equally blameworthy.

209
00:19:41,705 --> 00:19:42,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what Buddha said.

210
00:19:43,226 --> 00:19:46,948
[SPEAKER_00]: The only blameless one is the one that has uprooted greed, aversion, delusion.

211
00:19:48,189 --> 00:19:50,731
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot be... well, he can be blamed, but he's blameless.

212
00:19:50,831 --> 00:19:52,172
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that blame sticks to him.

213
00:19:52,952 --> 00:19:57,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's not partaking in the framework of the problem, which is desire.

214
00:19:59,276 --> 00:20:02,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

215
00:20:02,838 --> 00:20:05,019
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing you said a little while ago,

216
00:20:06,667 --> 00:20:24,198
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was in one of the YouTube videos that someone was asking about war, going to war, and that you were saying that, you know, somebody, to end hate, to end the war, somebody has to take the punch.

217
00:20:24,498 --> 00:20:26,519
Not retaliate.

218
00:20:26,639 --> 00:20:27,980
That's kind of what you were saying.

219
00:20:28,701 --> 00:20:29,841
Someone has to take it.

220
00:20:30,897 --> 00:20:33,038
There is going to be a fight somewhere along the line.

221
00:20:33,058 --> 00:20:34,258
Someone's going to come and punch you.

222
00:20:34,498 --> 00:20:38,499
But if you want the fight to stop, then you're going to... you can't retaliate.

223
00:20:38,719 --> 00:20:42,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to have to take that punch and just let it cool down.

224
00:20:42,801 --> 00:20:43,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone's going to do it.

225
00:20:43,481 --> 00:20:44,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

226
00:20:44,361 --> 00:20:45,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, that's, you know...

227
00:20:45,562 --> 00:20:46,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So what do you value?

228
00:20:47,302 --> 00:20:53,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you truly value peace, non-violence?

229
00:20:54,785 --> 00:20:55,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Are those your standards?

230
00:20:57,095 --> 00:21:01,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if pushed, your standards are to, well, I'm gonna have a last say or a last punch.

231
00:21:02,258 --> 00:21:05,559
[SPEAKER_00]: In which case, well, see, you don't truly value those things that you say you value.

232
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:15,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, injustice, injustice, violence, that is what the world is because world is rooted in desire.

233
00:21:15,404 --> 00:21:22,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Because world is made out of individuals whereby every single living being acts out of the desire.

234
00:21:24,234 --> 00:21:26,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why there are problems.

235
00:21:26,595 --> 00:21:28,957
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha said that himself.

236
00:21:29,357 --> 00:21:35,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Sons fight with fathers, fathers with mothers, with daughters, kings with kings, countries with the countries.

237
00:21:35,961 --> 00:21:36,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

238
00:21:36,501 --> 00:21:37,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of the sensual desire.

239
00:21:38,202 --> 00:21:38,522
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

240
00:21:38,562 --> 00:21:40,143
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the sole reason.

241
00:21:41,100 --> 00:21:48,144
[SPEAKER_00]: For all the conflict within a one family or multiple families or the entire kingdoms, it's actually the desires.

242
00:21:48,404 --> 00:21:48,804
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

243
00:21:48,945 --> 00:21:49,905
[SPEAKER_00]: People's desires.

244
00:21:50,605 --> 00:21:55,028
[SPEAKER_00]: They will have to reach a conflict because not all desires can be the same.

245
00:21:55,228 --> 00:22:05,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you truly value the end of conflict, means you must truly value ending of your own desire, not act out of desire for the ending of the conflict.

246
00:22:05,454 --> 00:22:06,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Becomes a contradiction in terms.

247
00:22:12,634 --> 00:22:16,836
[SPEAKER_01]: People can be unified with hate.

248
00:22:17,856 --> 00:22:19,877
[SPEAKER_01]: We are friends because we hate the same thing.

249
00:22:19,897 --> 00:22:20,877
[SPEAKER_00]: We hate the same thing, yeah.

250
00:22:21,017 --> 00:22:21,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Common enemy, yeah.

251
00:22:22,977 --> 00:22:23,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Common enemy.

252
00:22:23,678 --> 00:22:39,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when me and you, now, who are friends, because we hate the common enemy, we go and we destroy that enemy, and everything's fine, life goes back to normal, we come back into our homes, and what do we still have?

253
00:22:39,923 --> 00:22:40,763
We still have that

254
00:22:41,907 --> 00:22:49,909
value of hatred, of fighting, of killing, of fighting, fighting against that which is uncomfortable for us.

255
00:22:50,149 --> 00:22:50,849
[SPEAKER_00]: We don't want that.

256
00:22:50,869 --> 00:22:52,510
[SPEAKER_00]: That which is unacceptable to us.

257
00:22:52,550 --> 00:22:52,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

258
00:22:53,170 --> 00:22:54,130
[SPEAKER_01]: We have that attitude.

259
00:22:54,310 --> 00:22:54,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

260
00:22:54,550 --> 00:22:55,611
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, now, what's gonna happen?

261
00:22:56,891 --> 00:22:58,912
Now, we were friends, united in hate.

262
00:22:59,552 --> 00:23:00,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, we're enemy.

263
00:23:00,692 --> 00:23:01,812
We'll be fighting.

264
00:23:01,832 --> 00:23:04,893
[SPEAKER_01]: You're fighting with your father, you know, your friends, your neighbors.

265
00:23:05,253 --> 00:23:05,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

266
00:23:06,254 --> 00:23:11,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And before long, and before long, we might become someone else's unifying enemy.

267
00:23:15,631 --> 00:23:18,232
[SPEAKER_00]: And world goes back to normal for the time being?

268
00:23:18,352 --> 00:23:19,273
[SPEAKER_00]: How is that measured?

269
00:23:19,453 --> 00:23:24,735
[SPEAKER_00]: That's measured by, ah, I go back to my desires that I can fulfill.

270
00:23:26,976 --> 00:23:29,397
[SPEAKER_00]: My safety, my centrality, the access to the senses.

271
00:23:29,797 --> 00:23:31,457
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how people measure normality.

272
00:23:33,238 --> 00:23:37,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Through, basically, how much their desires regain access to things they want.

273
00:23:40,685 --> 00:23:46,708
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, then, you are, like, at peril of losing it.

274
00:23:46,808 --> 00:23:55,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the slightest change in circumstances, internally or externally, you will lose the access, means you will lose the safety, means you're back to where you were before.

275
00:23:55,752 --> 00:23:57,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all your victories meant nothing.

276
00:23:58,894 --> 00:23:59,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, turmoil.

277
00:24:00,094 --> 00:24:00,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

278
00:24:00,294 --> 00:24:00,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, turmoil.

279
00:24:01,895 --> 00:24:06,057
[SPEAKER_00]: And that comes down to that other thing, the possessions we spoke about before.

280
00:24:06,177 --> 00:24:07,938
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what makes you liable to suffer.

281
00:24:12,390 --> 00:24:21,174
[SPEAKER_01]: When it says in the Sutta, when the monks live in harmony like this, they make much merit.

282
00:24:21,394 --> 00:24:24,235
At that time, the monks live in a heavenly situation.

283
00:24:25,195 --> 00:24:28,977
This is the liberated mind of mudita.

284
00:24:29,837 --> 00:24:34,039
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind liberated, as in unconstricted, you know,

285
00:24:34,878 --> 00:24:46,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, not bound, not basically tethered to the weight of desires and possessions and sensual satisfactions and so on.

286
00:24:47,141 --> 00:25:01,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So that mind being liberated, being unconstricted, being so large and expansive, you know, when something occurs in the world, whatever happens,

287
00:25:02,290 --> 00:25:05,251
Natural disaster or an army comes and destroys you.

288
00:25:06,051 --> 00:25:16,853
That mind which has been liberated through acts of wholesome acts, through acts of peace, values of peace and so on, doesn't get moved.

289
00:25:17,033 --> 00:25:20,254
And wisdom doesn't get moved.

290
00:25:20,314 --> 00:25:22,094
That mind is harmonious.

291
00:25:22,154 --> 00:25:25,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a harmony, a samādhi which is moved.

292
00:25:25,905 --> 00:25:27,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's composed.

293
00:25:27,126 --> 00:25:35,331
[SPEAKER_00]: It's composed on the level that cannot be perturbed by changes in the world, no matter of the scale of the changes.

294
00:25:38,453 --> 00:25:40,714
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much what everybody wants.

295
00:25:41,535 --> 00:25:42,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people that go and...

296
00:25:43,405 --> 00:25:48,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Fight for desires and try to change things and, you know, modify the world.

297
00:25:49,227 --> 00:25:50,968
[SPEAKER_00]: They do because... for the same reason.

298
00:25:51,768 --> 00:25:56,410
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't want their minds to be perturbed by things that are disagreeable.

299
00:25:58,150 --> 00:26:04,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although it's a... it's a... it's a good intention, it's sort of aimed at the wrong things.

300
00:26:05,413 --> 00:26:08,554
[SPEAKER_00]: It's aimed at the wrong... it's aimed basically where the problem isn't.

301
00:26:13,904 --> 00:26:21,310
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's why the harmony that the Buddha talks about in those suttas, it's measured by the mind states of those individuals.

302
00:26:21,330 --> 00:26:27,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by the, as I said, by the etiquette and, you know, how often they bow to each other and softness of the words that they use.

303
00:26:28,376 --> 00:26:34,140
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's measured by the inability to have thoughts of ill will towards each other.

304
00:26:34,160 --> 00:26:42,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, generally, minds are so developed, expanded, through correct endurance, proper understanding, virtue and so on,

305
00:26:43,830 --> 00:26:49,072
[SPEAKER_00]: That they cannot turn towards each other with ill-will.

306
00:26:49,092 --> 00:26:50,653
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just impossible, inconceivable.

307
00:26:50,933 --> 00:26:53,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking up ill-will becomes inconceivable.

308
00:26:54,595 --> 00:26:55,975
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the perfect harmony.

309
00:26:56,175 --> 00:27:03,138
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the purest, the cleanest, the best of the assemblies, it's like the assembly of arahants, as we said.

310
00:27:03,158 --> 00:27:07,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it's the highest merit you get by helping that type of assembly.

311
00:27:08,920 --> 00:27:10,402
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically assembly of individuals.

312
00:27:10,502 --> 00:27:11,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a group.

313
00:27:11,563 --> 00:27:12,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahants are not a group.

314
00:27:12,884 --> 00:27:15,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It's every individual, fully purified.

315
00:27:15,900 --> 00:27:18,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, imagine the purity of a group, when there is no group.

316
00:27:18,923 --> 00:27:21,445
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just individuals free from greed, aversion, delusion.

317
00:27:22,285 --> 00:27:24,087
[SPEAKER_00]: That's THE divine assembly.

318
00:27:25,028 --> 00:27:31,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Assembly that has not projected its views on the level of others, and they, and us, and the group.

319
00:27:31,733 --> 00:27:34,476
[SPEAKER_00]: That group ownership that dissolves your individual responsibility.

320
00:27:34,816 --> 00:27:36,197
[SPEAKER_00]: That covers it up.

321
00:27:36,277 --> 00:27:37,959
[SPEAKER_00]: The group identity.

322
00:27:38,179 --> 00:27:40,641
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that exists in an assembly of arahants.

323
00:27:42,532 --> 00:27:43,813
[SPEAKER_00]: They are unmoved by any of that.

324
00:27:44,713 --> 00:27:49,995
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why they are the purest and the highest.

325
00:27:50,175 --> 00:27:54,337
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was just thinking about the word, you know, harmony.

326
00:27:54,357 --> 00:28:02,900
Like, wise, when two people are in harmony, you could also say that they are in agreement with each other.

327
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:03,880
Yeah.

328
00:28:05,481 --> 00:28:06,161
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no disagreement.

329
00:28:06,930 --> 00:28:09,992
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can, as with anything else, that can have two meanings.

330
00:28:10,052 --> 00:28:14,334
[SPEAKER_00]: It can have a worldly meaning, or it can have a meaning that pertains to the practice of dhamma.

331
00:28:14,354 --> 00:28:28,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're in harmony because we do the same things externally, or we are in harmony because both of our minds have developed in the same direction of uprooting ill will, lust, delusion, and everything else.

332
00:28:28,041 --> 00:28:29,982
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is also a type of harmony.

333
00:28:30,562 --> 00:28:35,265
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just not visible and measurable harmony as, you know, uniformity is.

334
00:28:41,135 --> 00:28:52,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the mind which is in agreement with reality.

335
00:28:52,943 --> 00:28:57,746
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a concord with reality.

336
00:28:59,187 --> 00:29:02,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in a disagreement with reality.

337
00:29:02,669 --> 00:29:04,630
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not taking feelings as mind.

338
00:29:04,983 --> 00:29:17,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, yeah, well, see, no, I mean, reality is not the best word there, because for a mind not developed, reality is its desire, is its greed, aversion, delusion.

339
00:29:17,230 --> 00:29:20,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the reality, very real reality.

340
00:29:24,634 --> 00:29:25,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what is a better word then?

341
00:29:26,955 --> 00:29:29,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't... why would you need a better word?

342
00:29:29,396 --> 00:29:30,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Just harmony.

343
00:29:31,437 --> 00:29:32,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the mind, yeah.

344
00:29:33,225 --> 00:29:47,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind, that it's basically in agreement with the principle of non-ill-will, or, you can say, desirelessness, and any of those terms, yeah, be in agreement with any of that, means you will be developed in that regard.

345
00:29:49,758 --> 00:29:51,939
[SPEAKER_00]: In tune with, you could say that, no?

346
00:29:52,220 --> 00:29:53,320
[SPEAKER_01]: In tune, exactly.

347
00:29:53,521 --> 00:29:56,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in tune with the dhamma.

348
00:29:57,295 --> 00:29:57,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

349
00:29:57,655 --> 00:30:09,844
[SPEAKER_00]: With the principles of dhamma, not in that some sort of esoterical sense, but simply with the principles of non-ill-will, non-aversion, non-lust, non-distraction, non-desire.

350
00:30:10,665 --> 00:30:16,169
[SPEAKER_00]: So, tune yourself to that, or to any of that for that matter, and you are heading in the right direction.

351
00:30:19,712 --> 00:30:26,777
[SPEAKER_00]: But when I say tune yourself to that, doesn't mean have wishful thinking that tunes you to that, but actually act accordingly.

352
00:30:29,992 --> 00:30:41,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, by being virtuous, by seeing the danger in the slightest fault, by being moderate in eating, by guarding of the sense doors, not giving in to sensual desires,

353
00:30:42,368 --> 00:30:52,095
[SPEAKER_00]: By not acting out of ill will, out of things that annoy you, by bearing it and enduring it, as Buddha said, the way you would endure the elements, then you will understand.

354
00:30:52,175 --> 00:30:54,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, and then, yes, you will be in tune.

355
00:30:54,817 --> 00:30:57,739
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a question of figuring it out so to be in tune.

356
00:30:57,799 --> 00:31:06,405
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is of enduring it rightly, putting in the work of non-acting and so on, not acting out and so on, and then you will be in tune.

357
00:31:06,465 --> 00:31:06,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

358
00:31:09,588 --> 00:31:12,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, then you will align yourself with those principles.

